            <ArticleTitle>Beware of the ventriculogallbladder shunt.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>274-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a case of distal end malfunction in a child with ventriculoabdominal shunt. The distal placement was in the gallbladder rather than the peritoneal cavity, as is usually the case with a ventriculoperitoneal shunt. Surgeons should be alerted to the possibility of distal terminus of a shunt to be other than the peritoneal cavity, as revision surgery in patients with ventriculogallbladder shunt requires exposure of the metal connector on the gallbladder wall to prevent biliary leakage.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
